<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pierre Dugua de Mons</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1558–<a href="/years/1628">1628</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Sieur de Mons</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pierre Dugua de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> was a Huguenot merchant and nobleman from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintonge">Saintonge</a> who held the French royal monopoly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. In 1604 he led the expedition, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain">Samuel de Champlain</a>, that wintered on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-croix">Saint Croix</a> Island, and in 1605 he founded Port-Royal, the first lasting French settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a>. He also sponsored Champlain's founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> in 1608.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-dugua-de-mons.jpg" alt="Pierre Dugua de Mons" width="246" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pierre_Dugua,_Sieur_de_Mons.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pierre Dugua de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> was born about 1558 at the Château de Mons in Royan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintonge">Saintonge</a>, to Guy and Claire Goumard Dugua. A Calvinist, he fought for Henri IV in the French wars of religion, and the king rewarded him with an annual pension of 1,200 crowns and the governorship of the town of Pons. He first travelled to north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> in 1599 with Pierre de Chauvin de Tonnetuit, and in 1600 went with him to Tadoussac.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1603 King Henry granted him the sole right to colonise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> between 40° and 60° North, a monopoly of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> and the title of Lieutenant General for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>; in return he promised to bring 60 new colonists each year. His expedition of 1604, backed by merchants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-malo">Saint-Malo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a>, sailed with 79 settlers, among them François Gravé Du Pont, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain">Samuel de Champlain</a>, the Baron de Poutrincourt, the priest Nicolas Aubry and the interpreter Mathieu de Costa, the first recorded black man in North America.</p><p>On St Croix Island many settlers died of the harsh winter and scurvy. Searching the coast for a better site, with the Native guide Panounias and his wife, the French met peoples who farmed in ways new to them. In 1606 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> captain Hendrick Lonck boarded two of his ships and took their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> and munitions. Port-Royal survived and prospered somewhat until 1607, when rival merchants protested against the monopoly and the king revoked it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Dugua ceded Port-Royal to Poutrincourt and turned to the St Lawrence valley. He never returned to the New World, but he sent Champlain to found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>, so playing a major part in founding the first permanent French colony in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. He was Lieutenant General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> until 1610. From 1610 to 1617 he governed the Protestant city of Pons, where he oversaw a grand staircase of six levels joining the upper and lower towns. He died in 1628 at the Château d'Ardenne in Fléac-sur-Seugne.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pierre Dugua, Sieur de Mons” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1604 <b>Pierre Dugua de Mons</b> and Samuel de Champlain wintered on Saint Croix Island, and in 1605 moved across the Bay of Fundy to found Port-Royal.</blockquote><a href="/state/acadia">Acadia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Château de Mons, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Château d'Ardennes, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.63,-1.03&amp;year=1603" aria-label="Château de Mons on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4526 555 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pierre Dugua de Mons"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4976 825h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4976 825h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.63,-1.03&amp;year=1603">What was here in 1603? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
